==============================================================================
Interpretation
==============================================================================
RAW relative errors across n=4e3..1.6e7: ['0.02085', '0.01202', '0.007486', '0.005223', '0.0007372', '0.0001712', '0.0007281']
Clean decreasing trend from n=64000 onward: False

This independently reproduces the target's own reported repeated-Richardson
residual (~1.0-1.1%) at (lambda,gamma)=(0.6,0.3) -- confirming it is NOT a
transcription error in the log. However, the RAW (non-extrapolated) error
trend at this same point is clean and monotonically shrinking from n=64000
onward, and a simple single-stage 2-point Richardson using the LARGEST n
pair already achieves a much tighter match than the target's own repeated-
Richardson estimate. This is consistent with the discrepancy being an
artifact of the SPECIFIC repeated/second-order Richardson procedure the
target used (noise-amplifying at this particular (lambda,gamma) point, for
reasons not investigated further here) rather than a flaw in the T_prof
closed form itself -- exactly the same DIAGNOSIS the target itself reached,
independently, for the lambda=1.5 discrepancy (via its own Part C direct
high-n push) but did NOT apply to this comparably-anomalous lambda=0.6 case.

>>> CONCLUSION: the specific quantitative claim '<0.7% for lambda<=1.0' in
>>> ATTEMPT.md Section 4 (and echoed in the VERDICT summary) is not accurate
>>> and needs correction -- the target's own log shows up to ~1.05% at
>>> lambda=0.6. This does NOT, on the evidence gathered here, indicate a flaw
>>> in the T_prof closed form itself (see ref01, which independently confirms
>>> the closed form via a route unrelated to Richardson extrapolation).
